★THE VERDICT OF THE PROBE
  ★ATTENTION TO ONE'S OWN DATA: the line «there is no mark at d=3» would be WRONG.  π₀ at d=3
  is trivial, BUT part D showed that the mark IS there — it is a BRANCH-mark, not a
  topological one.  So the verdict reads BOTH sources, not the more convenient one:

  | d | what is the mark | nature | half |
  | 2 | orientation (2 π₀-components) | TOPOLOGICAL | Λ² |
  | 3 | a pairing (3 branches, π₀=1)  | COMBINATORIAL | Sym² |
  | ≥4| neither one nor the other    | —            | no subject |

  ⟹ ★THERE IS NO ∀d-LAW IN THE SOUGHT FORM, and the reason is exact: the mark exists precisely where
  the configuration is combinatorially FORCED, that is, at d+1 ≤ 4.  At d+1=3 the forcing
  gives a discrete set (hence the topological mark), at d+1=4 — a decomposition into
  antipodal pairs (hence the combinatorial one).  At d+1≥5 there is no forcing at all:
  two structurally different exact solutions at n=5 have been exhibited.
  ⟹ Λ² at d=2 and Sym² at d=3 — are NOT two values of a function of d, but TWO DIFFERENT
  MECHANISMS on the only two d where the subject exists.  The question «which half ∀d»
  has no answer not for lack of computation, but through the EXHAUSTION of the subject.
  ★This is a named-boundary of the problem-front, not its solution — and it is reported precisely as such.
